Hi Andrew, thanks for the replies.

Agreed on the override policy as you describe it (the framework owns
the config, not the apps/connectors).

Following that ownership pov, I would like to bring back the Kafka
Streams case and the missing bit: identifying the instance of the
framework. For KS it would be valuable to know not only the framework
name but also the framework instance behind a client connection, so we
can reason about all the connections coming from one Streams instance
(embedded consumers/producer/admin). Streams already has a UUID for
this, the process id, and already sends it to the broker, but only on
individual paths (e.g, StreamsGroupHeartbeat). The process id is also
part of the default client.id, so the identifier is already there, but
this is where your ownership point matters: client.id is owned
by the user app, who can change it freely, vs. a framework id that
would be owned by the framework (just as the name and version). This
would be optional for the frameworks to set of course, just pointing
out that it's an extension to this intention of identifying the
framework, that seems relevant for KS. What do you think?

Thanks!
Lianet


