Hi all,

I have updated KIP-1165. To summarize, it now reflects what we have been
talking here in the past regarding object merging. It introduces object
consolidation, which is the process of transforming WAL segments into
classic Kafka logs:
https://cwiki.apache.org/confluence/spaces/KAFKA/pages/350783996/KIP-1165+O=
bject+Consolidation+for+Diskless
I'll update that thread and will probably start a new thread due to the
name change (object compaction -> object consolidation).

It still assumes ISR and doesn't talk about idempotency/transactional
support, that is still future work. I'll try to pick up JR3, JR4, JR5 and
JR6.2 next.

JR5.: true, also, I think it's arguable whether we need the full protocol
or can we just skip some parts of it. As you mention, all parts of a topic
is durably stored, we don't need to wait for local replicas to catch up as
the relevant data can be pulled from the object storage on the first fetch.
We may choose to do some caching though in advance instead of putting the
replica into the ISR immediately. KIP-1163 spells out 3 strategies:
1. Start building the local log from the earliest non-tiered offset.
2. Start building it from the latest available offset.
3. Start building it from some configurable amount, e.g. 100 MiB prior to
the current log end or earliest available offset, whichever is later.

Best,
Viktor

