Hi Andrew,

I'm not sure about using the instance ID to report connector type and
version. Concatenating the two and putting them in a single field removes
structure and forces extra work on users who want to, e.g., view aggregate
metrics for all versions of a connector. In addition, using it to
identify unique Kafka Streams processes makes sense, but (to me at least)
that means the intuitive use for it by Kafka Connect would be to report the
worker ID (i.e., the advertised URL) or the exact entity that the client is
used for (connector name for connectors, task ID for tasks). What do you
think about using the instance ID field to report the Connect worker ID
instead, since the connector name/task ID are already set in the default
client IDs used by Kafka Connect?

I wonder if there could be a sort of middle ground between "client" and
"framework" that would be a good fit for connector type/version--what do
you think about "application", as in "client.application.name" and
"client.application.version"? The Kafka Streams equivalents for these would
be the existing "application.id" property and a new "application.version"
property. This could also be independently useful for Kafka Streams
developers who want to track rollouts that update Streams configs and/or
topologies.

Also, I think that the Kafka Connect runtime should disallow setting any of
these properties, either in the worker config or in a connector config.

Cheers,

Chris

On Mon, Oct 5, 2026, 17:36 Andrew Schofield <schofielaj@apache.org> wrote:

> Hi Lianet,
> Thanks for your reply. I have posted an updated version of the KIP which
> adds a third config client.framework.id which a framework can use to set
> an additional framework-specific ID. For Kafka Streams, it's the process
> ID which can be used to group together connections which are part of the
> same logical application. For Kafka Connect, I suggest that the
> connector class and version are used. The validation rules for the
> framework ID are slightly different because the ID is derived from
> sources which the user does not directly control (such as the version
> string from a Kafka Connect connector).
>
> LM1: The ClientTelemetryContext interface is implemented by the broker
> with the intent that the metrics plugin can use it to obtain the context
> it needs to annotate the metrics. As such, there is no need to provide
> default implementations of the new methods.
>
> LM2: If an app is setting the configs intended for the framework to set
> the framework's values win.
>
> LM3: The new framework ID has a more lenient regex and the KIP describes
> how Kafka Connect forms the framework ID from the class name and the
> connector version.
>
> I think this update should answer most of the questions, and probably
> spark some more.
>
> Thanks,
> Andrew
