Hi,

After re-reading the KIP, I'm not sure whether bytes is better than count. Ev=
entually the prune must wait for "all" share partitions on the state partitio=
n to be snapshotted. That means users can hardly evaluate the retained log si=
ze from a per-partition threshold, no matter it is count or bytes.

On the other side, the concept of "per-group" is good to me, since it allows =
the low-traffic groups to snapshot more often.

So I'm wondering whether a per-group count config (plus raising the broker up=
per bound) is enough for this KIP.

Best,
Chia-Ping

