Subject: [jira] [Created] (KAFKA-21243) Producer: concurrent first sends can
 wait an extra retry.backoff.ms for a redundant metadata refresh
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: quoted-printable
X-JIRA-FingerPrint: 30527f35849b9dde25b450d4833f0394
From: "Viktor Klimin (Jira)" <jira@apache.org>
Reply-To: <dev@kafka.apache.org>

Viktor Klimin created KAFKA-21243:
-------------------------------------

             Summary: Producer: concurrent first sends can wait an extra re=
try.backoff.ms for a redundant metadata refresh
                 Key: KAFKA-21243
                 URL: https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/KAFKA-21243
             Project: Kafka
          Issue Type: Bug
          Components: producer=20
    Affects Versions: 3.9.2, 4.4.1
            Reporter: Viktor Klimin


h3. Issue

We run a producer with metadata.max.idle.ms at its default (5 min) and retr=
y.backoff.ms=3D750. When a topic has been idle longer than metadata.max.idl=
e.ms and several threads call send() at about the same time, some of them o=
ccasionally block for roughly 900 ms inside send(). That is about one retry=
.backoff.ms, even though the brokers are healthy and the first metadata res=
ponse comes back in a couple of milliseconds. In our case two threads that =
called send() within ~2 ms of each other were both stuck for ~897 ms and we=
re released together.
h3. Description
 # In KafkaProducer.{*}waitOnMetadata{*}() the thread first takes a snapsho=
t with metadata.fetch(), then calls metadata.requestUpdateForTopic(topic). =
These are two separate lock acquisitions. If the sender thread applies the =
first metadata response in between: ProducerMetadata.update() removes the t=
opic from newTopics, bumps updateVersion (V -> V+1), and sets lastRefreshMs=
 =3D now.
 # The waiting thread still has the old snapshot (no partitions for the top=
ic), so it does not take the fast path.
 # It calls {*}requestUpdateForTopic{*}(topic). The topic is no longer in n=
ewTopics, so this goes to {*}requestUpdate{*}(false). That only sets needFu=
llUpdate and returns V+1.
 # {*}awaitUpdate{*}(V+1) now needs a second metadata response, but a new r=
efresh is not allowed until *lastRefreshMs + retry.backoff.ms* (with jitter=
), so the thread sits there for ~{*}0.6-0.9 s at 750{*} ms.

The data the thread needs is already in the metadata at step 2, so the wait=
 is redundant. When the second response arrives, all the threads that hit t=
his window wake up together.
If requestUpdateForTopic() happens before the response is applied, the topi=
c is still in newTopics, requestUpdateForNewTopics() is used (no backoff), =
and the thread is released by the first response. So only the interleaving =
decides.
h3. Reproduction

The race is also reproducible with a real KafkaProducer.

Test setup: one producer, metadata.max.idle.ms=3D5000, metadata.max.age.ms=
=3D1000, retry.backoff.ms=3D750, brokers ~35 ms away. Every cycle: wait unt=
il the topic is evicted from the producer metadata, send from one "trigger"=
 thread, then send from 100 other threads at randomized instants spread ove=
r a ~20 ms window around the time the first metadata response is expected. =
Plain simultaneous start does not hit it, because all threads then arrive b=
efore the first response is applied; the arrivals have to be spread around =
the moment the response is applied.

Since the window is the time between {*}fetch{*}() and {*}requestUpdateForT=
opic{*}(), the example below shows the same.
