Subject: [jira] [Created] (KAFKA-21248) AsyncKafkaConsumer may auto-commit
 offsets for records not yet returned to the application
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: quoted-printable
X-JIRA-FingerPrint: 30527f35849b9dde25b450d4833f0394
From: "sanghyeok An (Jira)" <jira@apache.org>
Reply-To: <dev@kafka.apache.org>

sanghyeok An created KAFKA-21248:
------------------------------------

             Summary: AsyncKafkaConsumer may auto-commit offsets for record=
s not yet returned to the application
                 Key: KAFKA-21248
                 URL: https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/KAFKA-21248
             Project: Kafka
          Issue Type: Bug
            Reporter: sanghyeok An
            Assignee: sanghyeok An


{{AsyncKafkaConsumer}} may include offsets for records that have not yet be=
en returned to application code in a periodic auto-commit. If the commit su=
cceeds and the application terminates before processing those records, they=
 may be skipped after restart, violating at-least-once processing semantics=
.

This can happen even when the application processes every record returned b=
y one {{poll()}} before calling {{poll()}} again:
{code:java}
while (running) {
    ConsumerRecords<String, String> records =3D consumer.poll(timeout);

    for (ConsumerRecord<String, String> record : records) {
        process(record);
    }
} {code}
{{AsyncKafkaConsumer.poll()}} submits an {{AsyncPollEvent}} to the backgrou=
nd thread. When buffered records are available and the required conditions =
are satisfied, the application thread can collect those records before the =
event has finished processing.

Collecting records advances {{SubscriptionState.position}} to the next offs=
et to read. This update occurs before {{poll()}} returns to the application=
 and does not indicate that application code has finished processing the re=
cords.

However, the background thread calls {{subscriptions.allConsumed()}} when p=
rocessing the {{AsyncPollEvent}} to determine which offsets to auto-commit.=
 It can therefore use a position that the application thread has already ad=
vanced within the current {{poll()}} call.

Consider the following state for a single partition:
 * {{{}enable.auto.commit=3Dtrue{}}}, and the auto-commit interval has elap=
sed.
 * The application has finished processing all records through offset 99.
 * Both the committed offset stored on the broker and the current consumer =
position are 100.
 * Records at offsets 100 and 101 are available in the fetch buffer.

The following execution sequence is possible:
 # *Application code calls {{{}consumer.poll(){}}}.*

All records from the previous {{poll()}} have already been processed. The r=
ecords at offsets 100 and 101 have not yet been delivered to application co=
de.

 # *The application thread submits an {{{}AsyncPollEvent{}}}.*

Before the background thread processes this event, the application thread c=
an proceed to collect buffered records.

 # *The application thread collects the records at offsets 100 and 101 and =
advances the position to 102.*

At this point, {{poll()}} may not have returned yet. The internal consumer =
position is 102, but application code may still be waiting to receive the t=
wo records.

 # *The background thread processes the delayed {{{}AsyncPollEvent{}}}.*

Since the auto-commit interval has elapsed, it calls {{{}allConsumed(){}}}.=
 This method reads the current shared state and returns offset 102, which i=
s then included in the auto-commit request.

 # *The background thread sends the commit request, and the broker stores o=
ffset 102.*

This can complete while execution of the application thread is delayed. Con=
sequently, a resume position that skips these records can be stored before =
they have even been returned to the application.

 # *The application terminates before {{poll()}} returns.*

The records at offsets 100 and 101 have never been delivered to application=
 code, so {{process(record)}} has not been called for either record. Howeve=
r, offset 102 has already been committed on the broker.

 # *The same consumer group resumes consumption from the committed offset.*

Consumption starts at offset 102. The records at offsets 100 and 101 are sk=
ipped without ever having been processed by application code.

In this example, the safe offset to commit when the current {{poll()}} begi=
ns is 100. Reading the shared position when the event is processed instead =
produces offset 102, which includes the records collected by the current {{=
{}poll(){}}}.

The issue is a race between the position update inside {{poll()}} and the b=
ackground thread=E2=80=99s determination of the offsets to auto-commit. It =
does not depend on how quickly application code processes returned records:=
 the commit can advance before those records are delivered to the applicati=
on.



