sanghyeok An created KAFKA-21249:
------------------------------------

             Summary: Pre-rebalance auto-commit retries may commit offsets =
beyond unprocessed records
                 Key: KAFKA-21249
                 URL: https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/KAFKA-21249
             Project: Kafka
          Issue Type: Bug
            Reporter: sanghyeok An
            Assignee: sanghyeok An


When using the Java {{AsyncKafkaConsumer}} with {{group.protocol=3Dconsumer=
}} and {{{}enable.auto.commit=3Dtrue{}}}, a pre-rebalance auto-commit retry=
 may commit an offset beyond records that the application has not yet proce=
ssed.

When{{ CommitRequestManager.autoCommitSyncBeforeRebalanceWithRetries()}} re=
ceives a retriable error, it calls {{subscriptions.allConsumed()}} again an=
d replaces the existing request=E2=80=99s offset map. These offsets reflect=
 consumer positions advanced while collecting records for {{{}poll(){}}}, r=
ather than the application=E2=80=99s processing progress.

During a rebalance, record collection is blocked for partitions pending rev=
ocation, but records can still be returned from retained partitions. Althou=
gh the revocation and assignment sequence waits for the auto-commit to comp=
lete, the application thread does not wait for the entire commit retry sequ=
ence. Consequently, the following sequence is possible:
 # After the application finishes processing records through offset 99 in p=
artition P, a pre-rebalance auto-commit starts with offset 100.
 # While the initial commit request is pending, {{poll()}} returns records =
100=E2=80=93149 from retained partition P. The internal position advances t=
o 150, and the application starts processing the batch.
 # The initial commit request fails with a retriable error.
 # The retry logic reads the current position again, changing the offset to=
 commit from 100 to 150.
 # If offset 150 is committed and the process exits before processing the e=
ntire batch, the restarted consumer may resume from 150 and skip the remain=
ing unprocessed records.

This can occur even when a single application thread follows the sequence {=
{{}poll =E2=86=92 process all returned records =E2=86=92 next poll{}}}. It =
does not require the periodic auto-commit interval to expire.

An auto-commit retry should not commit beyond the application=E2=80=99s pro=
cessing progress. However, this retry path can include positions advanced b=
y records returned after the initial request was created. This may cause re=
cords to remain unprocessed by the consumer group after a failure.



