Hi Mickael and Paolo,
Thanks for the clarifications.

I=E2=80=99m working on PR #22865, which currently implements the client and=
 broker portion through DefaultSslEngineFactory. The implementation uses re=
flection for SSLParameters.setNamedGroups() and does not attempt to validat=
e named-group compatibility with the configured TLS version. I agree that t=
his should remain the responsibility of the JVM and SSL provider, consisten=
t with how cipher suites are handled.

I=E2=80=99ll also align the documentation with Java 27 and use x25519_mlkem=
768 in the examples.

There are two points I=E2=80=99d like to understand:

First, the JDK distinguishes an unset value from an empty named-groups arra=
y. An unset value preserves the provider defaults, while an explicitly empt=
y array means that no named groups are enabled. The KIP currently describes=
 the default as =E2=80=9CEmpty, the default JVM named groups are used,=E2=
=80=9D which could make these two cases ambiguous.

Would it make sense to define the behavior as follows?
  =20
   - Unset or null: use the JVM/provider defaults.
   - Explicitly empty list: disable named groups for that connection.
   - Non-empty list: apply the configured groups in preference order.

Second, would it be acceptable to keep PR #22865 focused on clients and bro=
kers, and implement the OAuth and Connect REST portions in separate follow-=
up PRs under the same KIP? This would keep each change smaller while still =
covering all the channels described by KIP-1376.

Thanks,
Arnab


Thanks and regardsArnab Nandy


On 2026/10/07 13:07:14 Mickael Maison wrote:
> Hi Paolo,
>
> 1. No I'm not referring to this API. As per the javadoc, the
> SSLParameters.setNamedGroups() API was added in Java 20.
> From a quick search it wasn't part of a JEP, here is the CSR:
> https://bugs.openjdk.org/browse/JDK-8291950
> https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/net/ss=
l/SSLParameters.html#setNamedGroups(java.lang.String[])
>
> 2. Good catch, fixed
>
> 3. My understanding is that roadmap is only for Oracle. I don't want
> to set expectations that other vendors will follow it or even backport
> that feature.
>
> 4. "Drop" means they are ignored. So if you set ssl.protocol to
> TLSv1.2 and a named group only available in TLSv1.3, the named group
> will be ignored.
> I propose to let the JVM handle it and possibly throw a TLS handshake
> exception at runtime if the configuration is invalid. This is based on
> these two reasons:
> - it matches the behavior of ciphers
> - checking the named groups and TLS version is likely to be brittle as
> new names are added/removed and the exact behavior may depend on the
> specific JVM
>
> Thanks,
> Mickael
>
>
>
> On Wed, Oct 7, 2026 at 12:11=E2=80=AFPM Paolo Patierno <pa...@gmail.com> =
wrote:
> >
> > Hi Mickael,
> > thanks for opening the KIP and starting such discussion.
> >
> > > Named groups are how the JVM describes TLS key exchange schemes. APIs=
 to
> > use named groups have been added in Java 20.
> > Are you referring to KEM API? Because JEP-452 <https://openjdk.org/jeps=
/452>
> > was done as part of Java 21 not 20.
> >
> > > This JEP was initially delivered in Java 26
> > Even in this case JEP-527 <https://openjdk.org/jeps/527> was delivered =
as
> > part of Java 27 not 26.
> >
> > > It's being backported by several implementations to earlier Java vers=
ions.
> > You can add a reference to the roadmap
> > <https://www.java.com/en/jre-jdk-cryptoroadmap.html> or you can find mo=
re
> > details on this link about the Oracle plan
> > https://blogs.oracle.com/java/post-quantum-cryptography-in-long-term-su=
pport-jdk-releases
> > so
> > that readers can have an idea about the older Java versions which are g=
oing
> > to get it (first one will be Java 25 at the end of October 2026).
> >
> > > The TLS version (set via ssl.protocol) gates what named groups are va=
lid
> > and automatically drops entries not compatible (like for ciphers).
> > What does exactly mean "drop" here? If I set TLS 1.2 for example but th=
en
> > in the named groups I add "X25519MLKEM768", does Kafka refuse the
> > configuration? Or it leaves to the JVM during the TLS handshake? (If yo=
u
> > have x25519 in the list it should fall back. It works for TLS 1.3 but n=
eed
> > to be confirmed for 1.2).
> >
> > Thanks,
> > Paolo
> >
> > On Tue, 6 Oct 2026 at 12:14, Mickael Maison <mi...@gmail.com>
> > wrote:
> >
> > > Hi,
> > >
> > > I'd like to start a discussion about KIP-1376 which proposes adding
> > > support for TLS named groups:
> > > https://cwiki.apache.org/confluence/x/dJXwGg
> > >
> > > Thanks,
> > > Mickael
