Subject: [jira] [Created] (KAFKA-21256) Assignment callback failure can
 stall reconciliation in the Streams protocol client
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: quoted-printable
X-JIRA-FingerPrint: 30527f35849b9dde25b450d4833f0394
From: "sanghyeok An (Jira)" <jira@apache.org>
Reply-To: <dev@kafka.apache.org>

sanghyeok An created KAFKA-21256:
------------------------------------

             Summary: Assignment callback failure can stall reconciliation =
in the Streams protocol client
                 Key: KAFKA-21256
                 URL: https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/KAFKA-21256
             Project: Kafka
          Issue Type: Bug
            Reporter: sanghyeok An
            Assignee: sanghyeok An


When Kafka Streams uses {{{}group.protocol=3Dstreams{}}}, a failure in the =
{{onTasksAssigned}} callback while processing a new active task assignment =
can leave the member unable to make further reconciliation progress.

The consumer=E2=80=99s partition assignment is updated before the callback =
runs, whereas {{currentAssignment}} is updated only after the callback succ=
eeds. A callback failure can therefore leave the actual consumer assignment=
 different from the last successfully reconciled assignment.

The next reconciliation attempt detects this mismatch and throws an {{{}Ill=
egalStateException{}}}. However, {{reconciliationInProgress}} has already b=
een set to {{{}true{}}}, and this exception path does not clear it. Subsequ=
ent attempts return immediately because they assume another reconciliation =
is still running.

=C2=A0

*Failure scenario*

Consider a member that is successfully processing assignment A and receives=
 a new assignment A+B:
 # Initially, both the consumer assignment and {{currentAssignment}} are A.
 # The consumer=E2=80=99s partition assignment is updated to A+B before inv=
oking the assignment callback.
 # The {{onTasksAssigned}} callback throws an exception. The consumer assig=
nment remains A+B, while {{currentAssignment}} remains A.
 # Callback failure handling clears the progress flag, allowing another rec=
onciliation attempt to begin.
 # The next attempt sets the progress flag again, detects the mismatch betw=
een the consumer assignment and {{{}currentAssignment{}}}, and throws an ex=
ception.
 # The exception leaves the progress flag set. This blocks both retries of =
the same target assignment and processing of newer target assignments.

=C2=A0
||Stage||Consumer Assignment||Last successufully reconciled assignment
(currentAssignment)||reconciliationInProgress||
|Initial State|A|A|false|
|New assignment applied (callback running)|A + B|A|true|
|After assignment callback failure handling|A + B|A|false|
|After the mismatch exception on the next attempt|A + B|A|true|
|Subsequent attempts|A + B|A|Remains true=C2=A0
(reconciliation returns immediately)|

=C2=A0

The same issue can occur when an assignment change includes revocation of e=
xisting tasks. For example, if A is revoked and the consumer assignment is =
changed to B before B=E2=80=99s assignment callback fails, the consumer ass=
ignment remains B while the completed assignment remains A. The next attemp=
t encounters the same mismatch.

This is particularly problematic for exceptions such as {{{}TaskCorruptedEx=
ception{}}}, which can be handled by an upper layer while execution continu=
es. The membership manager can remain stuck independently of recovery from =
the original error.

=C2=A0

*Impact*

The central issue is that an initial callback failure can escalate into a p=
ersistent state error that blocks all subsequent assignment processing.
 * *Further polling does not restore progress.* The retained progress flag =
prevents execution from reaching the reconciliation logic.
 * *New assignments cannot be processed.* Even if the broker provides a dif=
ferent target assignment, reconciliation remains blocked by the same flag.
 * *Processing of newly assigned partitions may never start while this stat=
e persists.* Partitions awaiting successful callback completion remain bloc=
ked from fetching.
 * *A live process does not imply assignment progress.* Thread execution an=
d heartbeats may continue after error handling while reconciliation remains=
 stalled.

As a result, even a transient or recoverable task error can prevent the mem=
ber from resuming processing through subsequent assignment changes.



