Subject: [jira] [Resolved] (KAFKA-21143) Server-only OAUTHBEARER listener
 with private JWKS CA triggers token login and fails broker startup
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit
X-JIRA-FingerPrint: 30527f35849b9dde25b450d4833f0394
From: "Chia-Ping Tsai (Jira)" <jira@apache.org>
Reply-To: <dev@kafka.apache.org>


     [ https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/KAFKA-21143?page=com.atlassian.jira.plugin.system.issuetabpanels:all-tabpanel ]

Chia-Ping Tsai resolved KAFKA-21143.
------------------------------------
    Fix Version/s: 4.5.0
       Resolution: Fixed

> Server-only OAUTHBEARER listener with private JWKS CA triggers token login and fails broker startup
> ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
>
>                 Key: KAFKA-21143
>                 URL: https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/KAFKA-21143
>             Project: Kafka
>          Issue Type: Bug
>          Components: security
>    Affects Versions: 4.2.2
>         Environment: Apache Kafka 4.2.0
>            Reporter: akshatha ramesh
>            Assignee: Muralidhar Basani
>            Priority: Critical
>             Fix For: 4.5.0
>
>
> h2. Problem
> A broker configured with an external _SASL_SSL/OAUTHBEARER_ listener can fail during startup when the broker is intended to act only as an OAuth token validator and the JWKS endpoint requires a private CA.
> h3. Topology
> {code:java}
> Inter-broker traffic:
> PLAINTEXT
> External listener:
> SASL_SSL + OAUTHBEARER
> External clients:
> obtain JWTs from an external IdP and present them to Kafka
> Kafka broker:
> validates incoming JWTs using the IdP JWKS endpoint
> does not need to obtain an OAuth token for itself
> {code}
> The JWKS endpoint uses HTTPS with a private CA, so trust configuration is supplied for the OAUTHBEARER listener:
> {code}
> listener.name.oam.oauthbearer.sasl.jaas.config=
> org.apache.kafka.common.security.oauthbearer.OAuthBearerLoginModule required
> ssl.truststore.location="/path/to/idp-ca.crt"
> ssl.truststore.type="PEM";
> listener.name.oam.oauthbearer.sasl.server.callback.handler.class=
> org.apache.kafka.common.security.oauthbearer.OAuthBearerValidatorCallbackHandler
> listener.name.oam.oauthbearer.sasl.oauthbearer.jwks.endpoint.url=
> https:///.../certs
> security.inter.broker.protocol=PLAINTEXT
> {code}
> h2. Actual behavior
> During broker startup, initialization of the external SASL listener reaches {{OAuthBearerLoginModule.login()}} and fails:
> {code:java}
> kafka.network.DataPlaneAcceptor.configure
> ...
> org.apache.kafka.common.network.SaslChannelBuilder.configure
> ...
> org.apache.kafka.common.security.oauthbearer.OAuthBearerLoginModule.login
> javax.security.auth.login.LoginException:
> An internal error occurred while retrieving token from callback handler
> {code}
> The failure occurs during listener initialization; an external client does not need to connect for the failure to occur.
> h2. Existing tokenless server-side behavior
> KAFKA-7462 addressed the case where {{OAuthBearerLoginModule}} is initialized on a broker even though OAUTHBEARER is not being used for inter-broker authentication.
> The default {{OAuthBearerUnsecuredLoginCallbackHandler}} detects the tokenless server-side case using:
> {code:java}
> if (moduleOptions.isEmpty()) {
> callback.token(null);
> return;
> }
> {code}
> {{OAuthBearerLoginModule}} accepts this tokenless login:
> {code:java}
> identifyToken();
> if (tokenRequiringCommit != null)
> identifyExtensions();
> else
> log.debug("Logged in without a token...");
> {code}
> Therefore, when there are no JAAS module options, the broker can initialize the OAUTHBEARER listener without obtaining an OAuth token for itself.
> Conceptually:
> {code:java}
> OAUTHBEARER listener
> +
> no broker OAuth client role
> +
> empty JAAS module options
> |
> v
> callback.token(null)
> |
> v
> OAuthBearerLoginModule accepts tokenless login
> |
> v
> listener starts
> {code}
> h2. Problem with a private-CA JWKS endpoint
> For a validation-only listener whose JWKS endpoint requires a private CA, the JAAS configuration contains TLS trust options:
> {code}
> ssl.truststore.location="/path/to/idp-ca.crt"
> ssl.truststore.type="PEM"
> {code}
> Consequently:
> {code:java}
> moduleOptions.isEmpty() == false
> {code}
> However, these options are present to establish TLS trust for the JWKS endpoint. They do not represent a requirement for the broker to obtain an OAuth access token.
> Because {{moduleOptions}} is no longer empty, {{OAuthBearerUnsecuredLoginCallbackHandler}} does not take the KAFKA-7462 tokenless path.
> It instead continues into its token construction path. Without the required unsecured-login principal configuration, token construction fails and the error is eventually surfaced from {{OAuthBearerLoginModule}} as:
> {code:java}
> javax.security.auth.login.LoginException:
> An internal error occurred while retrieving token from callback handler
> {code}
> h2. Expected behavior
> The following topology should be possible without requiring the broker to obtain an OAuth token for the external listener:
> {code:java}
> inter-broker = PLAINTEXT
> external listener = SASL_SSL/OAUTHBEARER
> broker OAuth role = validate incoming JWTs only
> JWKS endpoint = HTTPS with private CA
> {code}
> Providing TLS trust configuration required to access the JWKS endpoint should not, by itself, require broker-side OAuth token acquisition.
> h2. Possible gap
> The current {{moduleOptions.isEmpty()}} condition appears unable to distinguish between:
> {code:java}
> JAAS options representing broker-side OAuth login/token configuration
> {code}
> and:
> {code:java}
> JAAS options required only for TLS trust when accessing the JWKS endpoint
> {code}
> As a result, adding the TLS trust configuration needed for a private-CA JWKS endpoint prevents the existing tokenless server-side path from being taken.
> h2. Question
> Is there an intended supported configuration that allows JWKS TLS trust options to be supplied while retaining the tokenless server-side behavior introduced by KAFKA-7462?
> If not, should the tokenless server-side detection distinguish TLS/JWKS configuration from options that actually configure broker-side OAuth token creation or acquisition?
> h2. Related
> Confluent Kafka report:
> [https://github.com/confluentinc/kafka/issues/2160]
> KAFKA-7462:
> https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/KAFKA-7462



--
