Subject: [jira] [Created] (KAFKA-21279) Consumer and Streams group leave
 append failures can lose session timers and stall reassignment
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: quoted-printable
X-JIRA-FingerPrint: 30527f35849b9dde25b450d4833f0394
From: "sanghyeok An (Jira)" <jira@apache.org>
Reply-To: <dev@kafka.apache.org>

sanghyeok An created KAFKA-21279:
------------------------------------

             Summary: Consumer and Streams group leave append failures can =
lose session timers and stall reassignment
                 Key: KAFKA-21279
                 URL: https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/KAFKA-21279
             Project: Kafka
          Issue Type: Bug
            Reporter: sanghyeok An
            Assignee: sanghyeok An


When a member using {{group.protocol=3Dconsumer}} or {{group.protocol=3Dstr=
eams}} leaves its group, the coordinator cancels the member=E2=80=99s sessi=
on timer before persisting the member removal records.

If the subsequent local append to {{__consumer_offsets}} fails, the coordin=
ator=E2=80=99s snapshot rollback restores the member metadata, existing ass=
ignment, and ownership state. However, session timers are not managed by th=
e snapshot mechanism, so the canceled timer is not restored.

As a result, the coordinator retains a member that has already shut down as=
 the owner of Consumer partitions or Streams active tasks, but no longer ha=
s a session timer to expire that member. Even after the append failure is r=
esolved and other members continue sending successful heartbeats, the depar=
ted member=E2=80=99s partitions or tasks may remain unreleased, leaving pro=
cessing stalled.

The two protocols use different leave handlers, but share the same underlyi=
ng cause: timers are canceled before persistence and are not restored on ro=
llback.

=C2=A0

*Scenario*
 # Dynamic members A and B belong to the same group, with stable assignment=
s. In a Consumer group, A consumes P0 and B consumes P1. In a Streams group=
, A and B belong to different processes: A processes active task {{{}0_0{}}=
}, and B processes active task {{{}0_1{}}}.
 # A shuts down and sends a heartbeat with {{memberEpoch=3D-1}} using the c=
orresponding protocol. A sends no further heartbeats. For Streams, this is =
an ordinary member departure without requesting application-wide shutdown.
 # The coordinator generates A=E2=80=99s removal records and a group epoch =
update record, then calls {{cancelTimers()}} to cancel A=E2=80=99s timers b=
efore persisting the records.
 # The local append of the batch containing these records fails. The coordi=
nator remains ACTIVE without reloading and performs a snapshot rollback.
 # A=E2=80=99s member metadata and existing partition or active task owners=
hip are restored, but A=E2=80=99s session timer is not.
 # The cause of the append failure is resolved. B continues sending success=
ful heartbeats and processing its existing partitions or tasks, but B=E2=80=
=99s heartbeats refresh only B=E2=80=99s own session timer.
 # A is not removed even after its session timeout has elapsed. Consumption=
 of P0 remains stalled in the Consumer group, or processing of task {{0_0}}=
 remains stalled in the Streams group.

Even if a subsequent rebalance changes the target assignment to move the af=
fected partition or task to B, B may be unable to acquire it while the coor=
dinator still retains A=E2=80=99s current ownership.

For Streams, this scenario does not require a stateful topology, standby or=
 warm-up replicas, or a custom assignor.

=C2=A0

*Conditions that can cause the append failure*

One possible path is a reduction of {{max.message.bytes}} for {{__consumer_=
offsets}} between batch creation and append. Both group types use the same =
coordinator runtime persistence path, so this condition applies to both.

The coordinator runtime uses the size limit in effect when creating a batch=
. If the limit is subsequently reduced and the actual size of the batch con=
taining A=E2=80=99s leave records exceeds the new limit, the log append can=
 fail with {{{}RecordTooLargeException{}}}.

In this failure path, rolling back the batch does not necessarily reload th=
e coordinator. Restoring the size limit allows subsequent writes to succeed=
, but does not restore A=E2=80=99s canceled session timer.

=C2=A0

*Impact*

In Consumer groups, consumption of the departed member=E2=80=99s partitions=
 may remain stalled. In Streams groups, input processing for the departed m=
ember=E2=80=99s active tasks may remain stalled. Other members can continue=
 processing the partitions or tasks they already own, so the issue does not=
 necessarily stop processing across the entire group.

Reloading the coordinator can register the members=E2=80=99 session timers =
again. This is therefore not permanent log corruption, but restoring succes=
sful writes and waiting for the session timeout are insufficient to guarant=
ee that the affected partitions or tasks will be released.



