Hi Mickael,

Thanks for the KIP!
This is necessary to protect connections in kafka.

LC1: "These tests will be skipped when run on a JVM that does not support
named groups. The CI already runs with Java 25, so this will be effectively
tested."
I'm wondering if we can run tests with all java versions, but expect to
fail when running on unsupported versions? If we only test java 25, we
can't test the unsupported java version with ConfigExceptions, right?

LC2: About the jvm "jdk.tls.namedGroups" system properties:
LC2.1. What if current users already set "jdk.tls.namedGroups=3Dx25519"
system properties, and after this KIP, it also sets
"ssl.named.groups=3Dx448", then I think the jvm system properties will be
skipped, right? Do you think this will cause some unexpected security
problems? Maybe when setting "ssl.named.groups", we should check if
"jdk.tls.namedGroups" system properties are set or not?
LC2.2. In the compatibility section, I think we should add some
explanations about how we honor "ssl.named.groups" and
"jdk.tls.namedGroups" when each is set or not set.


Thank you,
Luke

On Sun, Oct 11, 2026 at 10:49=E2=80=AFPM Arnab Nandy via dev <dev@kafka.apa=
che.org>
wrote:

> Hi Mickael,
>
> Thanks, that clarifies both points.
>
> I=E2=80=99ll update PR #22865 so that ssl.named.groups follows the same b=
ehavior
> as ssl.cipher.suites: when the configuration is empty or unset, Kafka wil=
l
> not call SSLParameters.setNamedGroups() and the JVM/provider defaults wil=
l
> be preserved. A non-empty value will be applied in the configured
> preference order.
>
> The current PR will be more focused on the client and broker
> implementation, and handle OAuth and Connect REST support in separate
> follow-up PRs under KIP-1376.
>
> Also update the documentation and examples to use Java 27 and
> x25519_mlkem768.
>
> And sorry for this duplication on a separate thread. I'm using ASF Mailin=
g
> List first time so misjudged how it works, adding the link here for the
> context of my reply.
>
> https://lists.apache.org/thread/olv28ft7m05obm01b8hj19mld2lzhc9d
>
> Thanks,
> Arnab
>
> On 2026/10/07 13:07:14 Mickael Maison wrote:
> > Hi Paolo,
> >
> > 1. No I'm not referring to this API. As per the javadoc, the
> > SSLParameters.setNamedGroups() API was added in Java 20.
> > From a quick search it wasn't part of a JEP, here is the CSR:
> > https://bugs.openjdk.org/browse/JDK-8291950
> >
> https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/net/ss=
l/SSLParameters.html#setNamedGroups(java.lang.String[])
> >
> > 2. Good catch, fixed
> >
> > 3. My understanding is that roadmap is only for Oracle. I don't want
> > to set expectations that other vendors will follow it or even backport
> > that feature.
> >
> > 4. "Drop" means they are ignored. So if you set ssl.protocol to
> > TLSv1.2 and a named group only available in TLSv1.3, the named group
> > will be ignored.
> > I propose to let the JVM handle it and possibly throw a TLS handshake
> > exception at runtime if the configuration is invalid. This is based on
> > these two reasons:
> > - it matches the behavior of ciphers
> > - checking the named groups and TLS version is likely to be brittle as
> > new names are added/removed and the exact behavior may depend on the
> > specific JVM
> >
> > Thanks,
> > Mickael
> >
> >
> >
> > On Wed, Oct 7, 2026 at 12:11=E2=80=AFPM Paolo Patierno <pa...@gmail.com=
> wrote:
> > >
> > > Hi Mickael,
> > > thanks for opening the KIP and starting such discussion.
> > >
> > > > Named groups are how the JVM describes TLS key exchange schemes.
> APIs to
> > > use named groups have been added in Java 20.
> > > Are you referring to KEM API? Because JEP-452 <
> https://openjdk.org/jeps/452>
> > > was done as part of Java 21 not 20.
> > >
> > > > This JEP was initially delivered in Java 26
> > > Even in this case JEP-527 <https://openjdk.org/jeps/527> was
> delivered as
> > > part of Java 27 not 26.
> > >
> > > > It's being backported by several implementations to earlier Java
> versions.
> > > You can add a reference to the roadmap
> > > <https://www.java.com/en/jre-jdk-cryptoroadmap.html> or you can find
> more
> > > details on this link about the Oracle plan
> > >
> https://blogs.oracle.com/java/post-quantum-cryptography-in-long-term-supp=
ort-jdk-releases
> > > so
> > > that readers can have an idea about the older Java versions which are
> going
> > > to get it (first one will be Java 25 at the end of October 2026).
> > >
> > > > The TLS version (set via ssl.protocol) gates what named groups are
> valid
> > > and automatically drops entries not compatible (like for ciphers).
> > > What does exactly mean "drop" here? If I set TLS 1.2 for example but
> then
> > > in the named groups I add "X25519MLKEM768", does Kafka refuse the
> > > configuration? Or it leaves to the JVM during the TLS handshake? (If
> you
> > > have x25519 in the list it should fall back. It works for TLS 1.3 but
> need
> > > to be confirmed for 1.2).
> > >
> > > Thanks,
> > > Paolo
