Hi Aditya,

I'm sharing my POV based on implementation PR #22865.

For AK2, DefaultSslEngineFactory resolves SSLParameters.setNamedGroups() on=
ce during class initialization and stores it in a static final Method. The =
method is invoked only when ssl.named.groups is non-empty. Using reflection=
 avoids a compile-time dependency on the Java 20 API while Kafka clients co=
ntinue to support earlier Java versions.

For AK3, the Java-version check and InvalidConfigurationException are behav=
ior of the default SSL engine factory. A custom ssl.engine.factory.class ma=
y support named groups through another mechanism, so I don=92t think Kafka =
should require every custom implementation to follow the reflection-based b=
ehavior. It would be useful for the KIP to make the scope of that guarantee=
 explicit.

For AK5, invalid group names supplied to setNamedGroups() are already wrapp=
ed in an InvalidConfigurationException. If both endpoints support the confi=
gured groups individually but have no group in common, that is discovered d=
uring the handshake and remains an SSLHandshakeException from JSSE. Listing=
 only the local JVM=92s supported groups may be misleading because Kafka do=
es not know the peer=92s supported groups at that point.

For AK6, the negative test has now been added to PR #22865. On Java version=
s below 20, it verifies that a non-empty ssl.named.groups causes an Invalid=
ConfigurationException. The Java 17 CI lane will exercise that path.

Regarding AK7, I agree that a multi-release JAR is technically possible, bu=
t it introduces additional build and shading complexity for a single API ca=
ll. Reflection appears to be the simpler and more robust option here.

Thanks,
Arnab

On 2026-10-16 15:30 UTC Aditya Kousik via dev wrote:
> Hi Mickael,
>
> Thanks for the KIP. It=92s good to see Kafka take on the PQC world; I had=
 a few comments.
>
> AK1: nit: but the Status is still marked as Draft and the Discussion Link=
 is still TBD
>
> AK2: The reflection mechanism could use some details. Are we storing a Me=
thodHandle reference when DefaultSslEngineFactory initializes in a static b=
lock?
>
> AK3: If the contract is to throw ConfigException because of missing names=
 group support on the jdk, there=92s nothing in the SslEngineFactory to hav=
e other engines follow the spec. ssl.engine.factory.class for other engines=
 won=92t adhere to this, and may break silently after launch.
>
> AK4: Should we file a JIRA in conjunction to deprecate/cleanup the reflec=
tion once all clients and servers update their minimum supported version >=
=3D 20?
>
> AK5:
> > a TLS connection can only be established when the client and server sha=
re at least one named group
>
> I see in other replies than this would this be an sslhandshakexception th=
at the jdk SSLEngine throws. Would it be worth adding a custom KafkaExcepti=
on that suggests to users on the supported named groups in the current VM v=
ia SSLContext.getSupportedSSLParameters().getNamedGroups(). We sometimes do=
 this when serialization fails, max poll interval elapses etc.
>
> AK6: On Testing, there=92s a negative test that we=92re missing. We can e=
nsure assertThrows(ConfigException.class) on jdk versions < 20 which maybe =
a lot of the clients out there.
>
> AK7: I think for the simplicity alone, reflection is what we might settle=
 on. But I wanted to call out that there=92s technically another approach t=
hat might get us there without it.
> We could do a multi-release JAR which has been available since jdk9. We c=
an create a custom java20 source set and have gradle include it in the buil=
d.
> We can define a plain class on the base layer as
> ```java
> public class NamedGroupSupport {
> public boolean supported() { return false; }
> public void apply(SSLParameters sslParams, String[] groups) { throw new U=
nsupportedOperationException(); }
> }
> ```
> And in the java20 source set, have the right implementation. We also need
> ```groovy
> jar {
> into(=91META-INF/versions/20=92) { from sourceSets.java20.output }
> manifest { attributes =91Multi-Release=92: =91true=92 }
> }
> ```
> With the above in addition to a custom java20 source set with options.rel=
ease =3D 20 could get us there for free.
>
> But the cost is the build complexity, if someone shades the kafka-clients=
 JAR they lose support. Also for clients who fork and build their own JARs =
this would not be preferable. So overall, reflection is the simpler answer =
I think but an alternative nonetheless to reject imo.
