Hi Mickael,

Thank you for the KIP.

I wonder if we should also introduce a metric to identify the named =
group a TLS connection is using. That would be of use to operators =
who=E2=80=99d like to track PQC adoption across clients connecting to =
their Kafka cluster. I think the selector metrics track TLS protocol and =
cipher information so there=E2=80=99s some precedent for it. As far as =
JDK support goes, I think we=E2=80=99d be able to get this information =
from JDK 28 onwards [0] (or earlier if a vendor backports it).


[0]: https://bugs.openjdk.org/browse/JDK-8388519

Regards,
Gaurav Narula

> On 16 Oct 2026, at 19:47, Arnab nandy via dev <dev@kafka.apache.org> =
wrote:
>=20
> Hi Aditya,
>=20
> I'm sharing my POV based on implementation PR #22865.
>=20
> For AK2, DefaultSslEngineFactory resolves =
SSLParameters.setNamedGroups() once during class initialization and =
stores it in a static final Method. The method is invoked only when =
ssl.named.groups is non-empty. Using reflection avoids a compile-time =
dependency on the Java 20 API while Kafka clients continue to support =
earlier Java versions.
>=20
> For AK3, the Java-version check and InvalidConfigurationException are =
behavior of the default SSL engine factory. A custom =
ssl.engine.factory.class may support named groups through another =
mechanism, so I don=E2=80=99t think Kafka should require every custom =
implementation to follow the reflection-based behavior. It would be =
useful for the KIP to make the scope of that guarantee explicit.
>=20
> For AK5, invalid group names supplied to setNamedGroups() are already =
wrapped in an InvalidConfigurationException. If both endpoints support =
the configured groups individually but have no group in common, that is =
discovered during the handshake and remains an SSLHandshakeException =
from JSSE. Listing only the local JVM=E2=80=99s supported groups may be =
misleading because Kafka does not know the peer=E2=80=99s supported =
groups at that point.
>=20
> For AK6, the negative test has now been added to PR #22865. On Java =
versions below 20, it verifies that a non-empty ssl.named.groups causes =
an InvalidConfigurationException. The Java 17 CI lane will exercise that =
path.
>=20
> Regarding AK7, I agree that a multi-release JAR is technically =
possible, but it introduces additional build and shading complexity for =
a single API call. Reflection appears to be the simpler and more robust =
option here.
>=20
> Thanks,
> Arnab
>=20
> On 2026-10-16 15:30 UTC Aditya Kousik via dev wrote:
>> Hi Mickael,
>>=20
>> Thanks for the KIP. It=E2=80=99s good to see Kafka take on the PQC =
world; I had a few comments.
>>=20
>> AK1: nit: but the Status is still marked as Draft and the Discussion =
Link is still TBD
>>=20
>> AK2: The reflection mechanism could use some details. Are we storing =
a MethodHandle reference when DefaultSslEngineFactory initializes in a =
static block?
>>=20
>> AK3: If the contract is to throw ConfigException because of missing =
names group support on the jdk, there=E2=80=99s nothing in the =
SslEngineFactory to have other engines follow the spec. =
ssl.engine.factory.class for other engines won=E2=80=99t adhere to this, =
and may break silently after launch.
>>=20
>> AK4: Should we file a JIRA in conjunction to deprecate/cleanup the =
reflection once all clients and servers update their minimum supported =
version >=3D 20?
>>=20
>> AK5:
>>> a TLS connection can only be established when the client and server =
share at least one named group
>>=20
>> I see in other replies than this would this be an =
sslhandshakexception that the jdk SSLEngine throws. Would it be worth =
adding a custom KafkaException that suggests to users on the supported =
named groups in the current VM via =
SSLContext.getSupportedSSLParameters().getNamedGroups(). We sometimes do =
this when serialization fails, max poll interval elapses etc.
>>=20
>> AK6: On Testing, there=E2=80=99s a negative test that we=E2=80=99re =
missing. We can ensure assertThrows(ConfigException.class) on jdk =
versions < 20 which maybe a lot of the clients out there.
>>=20
>> AK7: I think for the simplicity alone, reflection is what we might =
settle on. But I wanted to call out that there=E2=80=99s technically =
another approach that might get us there without it.
>> We could do a multi-release JAR which has been available since jdk9. =
We can create a custom java20 source set and have gradle include it in =
the build.
>> We can define a plain class on the base layer as
>> ```java
>> public class NamedGroupSupport {
>> public boolean supported() { return false; }
>> public void apply(SSLParameters sslParams, String[] groups) { throw =
new UnsupportedOperationException(); }
>> }
>> ```
>> And in the java20 source set, have the right implementation. We also =
need
>> ```groovy
>> jar {
>> into(=E2=80=98META-INF/versions/20=E2=80=99) { from =
sourceSets.java20.output }
>> manifest { attributes =E2=80=98Multi-Release=E2=80=99: =E2=80=98true=E2=
=80=99 }
>> }
>> ```
>> With the above in addition to a custom java20 source set with =
options.release =3D 20 could get us there for free.
>>=20
>> But the cost is the build complexity, if someone shades the =
kafka-clients JAR they lose support. Also for clients who fork and build =
their own JARs this would not be preferable. So overall, reflection is =
the simpler answer I think but an alternative nonetheless to reject imo.
>>=20
>> Thanks,
>> Aditya Kousik
>>=20
>> On 2026-10-06 10:14 UTC Mickael Maison wrote:
>>> Hi,
>>>=20
>>> I'd like to start a discussion about KIP-1376 which proposes adding
>>> support for TLS named groups:
>>> https://cwiki.apache.org/confluence/x/dJXwGg
>>>=20
>>> Thanks,
>>> Mickael
>>>=20
>>=20


