sanghyeok An created KAFKA-21300:
------------------------------------

             Summary: Streams group task ownership index is not rolled back=
, blocking valid task assignments
                 Key: KAFKA-21300
                 URL: https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/KAFKA-21300
             Project: Kafka
          Issue Type: Bug
            Reporter: sanghyeok An
            Assignee: sanghyeok An


When the Streams group coordinator rolls back a task assignment change, mem=
ber assignments and the process ownership index for standby and warm-up tas=
ks can become inconsistent.

In addition to per-member assignments, the coordinator maintains a set of p=
rocess IDs that own each task. This index prevents the same task from being=
 assigned to different members within the same process in conflicting roles=
.

Although the ownership index is stored in a {{{}TimelineHashMap{}}}, its va=
lues are ordinary {{HashSet}} instances that are mutated in place. Revertin=
g to a snapshot therefore does not restore the previous contents of these s=
ets. Member assignments are restored correctly, but the index may retain ow=
nership from a rolled-back assignment or lose ownership that should have be=
en restored.

A representative scenario involves a log append failure while adding a stan=
dby owner to a task that already has one:
 # P1 owns active task T, P3 owns standby task T, and P2 has no assigned ta=
sks.
 # Increasing the number of standby replicas produces a target assignment t=
hat also assigns standby T to P2.
 # While processing P2=E2=80=99s heartbeat, the coordinator replays the cur=
rent-assignment record in memory, changing the standby ownership index to {=
{{}{P3, P2}{}}}.
 # The log append fails and the coordinator rolls back. P2=E2=80=99s assign=
ment becomes empty again, but P2 remains in the ownership index.
 # If P1 and P3 subsequently leave and P2 needs to receive active T, the co=
ordinator blocks the assignment because it believes another member in proce=
ss P2 still owns standby T.

No actual owner exists to release the task. The member can continue sending=
 successful heartbeats while retaining an empty assignment and remaining in=
 {{{}UNRELEASED_TASKS{}}}. For an application with a single task, this can =
halt input processing.

Removing the original member and rejoining with the same process ID may not=
 resolve the issue. Since the original member=E2=80=99s assignment is empty=
 after rollback, member removal does not clean up the stale ownership entry=
. Restarting an application with persistent stores while preserving its sta=
te directory can reuse the same process ID and meet this condition.

The reverse inconsistency can also occur. If a standby or warm-up owner is =
removed and that change is rolled back, the member=E2=80=99s assignment is =
restored, but its process ID may remain absent from the ownership index. Th=
e check that should prevent another member in the same process from receivi=
ng the active task can then incorrectly allow the assignment.

The impact can persist after such a rollback while the same coordinator sta=
te remains loaded. Reconstructing coordinator state from persisted member r=
ecords also rebuilds the ownership index.



