Subject: [jira] [Created] (KAFKA-21302) Streams group joins rejected due to
 copartition topic ordering differences
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: quoted-printable
X-JIRA-FingerPrint: 30527f35849b9dde25b450d4833f0394
From: "sanghyeok An (Jira)" <jira@apache.org>
Reply-To: <dev@kafka.apache.org>

sanghyeok An created KAFKA-21302:
------------------------------------

             Summary: Streams group joins rejected due to copartition topic=
 ordering differences
                 Key: KAFKA-21302
                 URL: https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/KAFKA-21302
             Project: Kafka
          Issue Type: Bug
            Reporter: sanghyeok An
            Assignee: sanghyeok An


With {{{}group.protocol=3Dstreams{}}}, a new application instance can fail =
to join its group even when it uses the same logical topology, depending on=
 the order in which source topics are supplied.

For example, two instances with the same {{application.id}} construct their=
 topologies as follows:
 * Instance A joins {{stream(List.of("Aa", "BB"))}} with a stream from topi=
c {{{}z{}}}.
 * Instance B joins {{stream(List.of("BB", "Aa"))}} with a stream from topi=
c {{{}z{}}}.

All three topics have the same partition count. Node names, join settings, =
and all other configuration are identical. Only the source topic order diff=
ers, so both topologies have the same copartition constraints.

The client sorts the source topic list in the heartbeat, producing {{[Aa, B=
B, z]}} for both instances. However, copartition indexes are collected in S=
et iteration order, so A can send {{[0, 1, 2]}} while B sends {{{}[1, 0, 2]=
{}}}.

After A joins and its topology is stored, the coordinator treats B=E2=80=99=
s different index ordering as a topology change and returns:

=C2=A0

{{INVALID_REQUEST: Topology updates are not supported yet.}}

B=E2=80=99s client treats this error as fatal, transitions from {{JOINING}}=
 to {{{}FATAL{}}}, and stops sending heartbeats. As a result, adding or rep=
lacing an instance can fail despite the instances using logically identical=
 topologies.

This occurs when the copartition list ordering actually differs. Reversing =
an arbitrary source topic list does not necessarily trigger the issue. In t=
his example, {{Aa}} and {{BB}} have the same Java string hash code, allowin=
g their input order to affect Set iteration order.



