Gavin Wang created KAFKA-21306:
----------------------------------

             Summary: Streams group: a static member that rejoins from a ne=
w process can put a task on that process twice
                 Key: KAFKA-21306
                 URL: https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/KAFKA-21306
             Project: Kafka
          Issue Type: Bug
          Components: streams
            Reporter: Gavin Wang
            Assignee: Gavin Wang


h3. Problem
In a streams group (KIP-1071), one process must never hold the same task tw=
ice. CurrentAssignmentBuilder says "a single process must never run the sam=
e task twice". The reconciler enforces this only when it grants a task: the=
 isUnreleased*Task checks run on tasks pending assignment, and a task the m=
ember already holds is kept without any check.

A static member that rejoins under a new member ID bypasses that check:
# The rejoin is accepted once the old member has left with epoch -2. GroupM=
etadataManager#getOrMaybeCreateStaticStreamsGroupMember copies the old memb=
er, including its assigned tasks.
# The heartbeat then sets the process ID from the request. Nothing compares=
 it with the old process ID.
# If the rejoin comes from a different process, the copied tasks move to th=
at process. If another member there already holds one of them as a standby =
or warm-up task, that process now holds the task twice.

The new target assignment that the process ID change triggers does not remo=
ve the duplicate. The sticky assignor keeps the active task on the rejoined=
 member. The standby on the sibling member is revoked, but it stays until t=
hat member acknowledges the revocation.

h3. When it happens
* Kafka Streams with static membership: each stream thread uses group.insta=
nce.id "<id>-<threadIdx>". Member IDs are new on every restart, so a restar=
ted thread always takes the rejoin path above.
* The process ID changes across a restart when the topology has no persiste=
nt stores (StateDirectory#initializeProcessId creates a random UUID on ever=
y start), or when the state directory is lost or the instance moves to anot=
her host.
* Sequence: an instance with two threads restarts under a new process ID B.=
 Thread 2 rejoins first and is given the standby of task t, which is allowe=
d because active t is still on the old process. Thread 1 rejoins and inheri=
ts active t. Process B now holds t both as an active task and as a standby =
task.

h3. Impact
Both threads of the same instance are told to run t at the same time. This =
lasts until the next target assignment revokes the standby and the second t=
hread acknowledges it: about one heartbeat interval, plus up to the assignm=
ent interval. From reading the client code:
* With persistent stores, the task-directory lock makes the new active task=
 retry until the standby releases it, so t stalls.
* Without persistent stores, which is exactly the case where the process ID=
 changes on every restart, the lock is a no-op. Both copies run, which dupl=
icates memory and restore work.

h3. Reproduction
The test below fails on trunk (dfd3dcab07) at the final assertion: process-=
B is both the active owner and a standby holder of s1_0.

