Subject: [jira] [Created] (KAFKA-21319) Share partition read_committed
 acquire throws "Batch record not found" when the start offset is inside an
 aborted transaction batch
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: quoted-printable
X-JIRA-FingerPrint: 30527f35849b9dde25b450d4833f0394
From: "Charles-Eddy (Jira)" <jira@apache.org>
Reply-To: <dev@kafka.apache.org>

Charles-Eddy created KAFKA-21319:
------------------------------------

             Summary: Share partition read_committed acquire throws "Batch =
record not found" when the start offset is inside an aborted transaction ba=
tch
                 Key: KAFKA-21319
                 URL: https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/KAFKA-21319
             Project: Kafka
          Issue Type: Bug
          Components: core
    Affects Versions: 4.5.0
            Reporter: Charles-Eddy
         Attachments: 3-batch-record-not-found-read-committed.patch

I found this while prototyping key-based ordering for share groups. It repr=
oduces on plain trunk.

With share.isolation.level=3Dread_committed, filterAbortedTransactionalAcqu=
iredRecords looks up each aborted batch of the fetched data with cachedStat=
e.floorEntry(batch.baseOffset()). If the share partition start offset lies =
inside an aborted batch, for example after DeleteRecords to a mid-batch off=
set or, from reading the code, when a by_duration auto offset reset lands t=
here, the cached batch starts at the start offset, so nothing in the cache =
starts at or below the aborted batch base offset, and fetchSubMap throws af=
ter the records were acquired:


{code:java}
java.lang.IllegalStateException: Batch record not found. The request batch =
offsets are not found in the cache.
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 at kafka.server.share.SharePartition.fetchSubMap(SharePartiti=
on.java:3309)
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 at kafka.server.share.SharePartition.filterAbortedTransaction=
alAcquiredRecords(SharePartition.java:3207)
  =C2=A0 at kafka.server.share.SharePartition.acquire(SharePartition.java:8=
61){code}

The fetched records stay ACQUIRED and are not delivered, and each retry aft=
er the lock expires fails the same way and uses a delivery attempt.

With share.delivery.count.limit=3D2, the committed records after the aborte=
d batch are archived without ever being delivered. With the default limit o=
f 5, the committed records are delivered in the end, after 7 failed fetches=
, once delivery throttling has archived the aborted records one by one.=C2=
=A0

The attached patch adds a test that reproduces it: SharePartitionTest#testA=
cquireReadCommittedWhenStartOffsetIsWithinAbortedTransactionBatch.

Looking up max(batch.baseOffset(), startOffset) instead of batch.baseOffset=
(), or skipping aborted batches that do not overlap the cached range, shoul=
d fix it.



