Hello Andrew,

seems the KIP did make significant progress. Nice! -- Overall LGTM, even 
if it seems there is still some stuff we need to work our for Connect; I 
am not super familiar with Connect details but it seems Chris might have 
a point about worker IDs? Not sure about connect tasks though, which 
seems to be too low level? Chris, correct me if I am wrong, but a single 
consumer/producer would be shared across multiple sink/source tasks, 
right? If there is not a 1:1 mapping between task and client it seems 
task information would not be something we want to add (KS also has 
task, and a 1:N mapping to clients, and I don't see any advantage to add 
KS-task information to a connection -- the assigned tasks can also 
change during the live time of a connect for KS, and I would assume it's 
the same for Connect?)



Bunch of nits:


> The broker limits the length (249 characters)

This makes sense, but could imply that long package names cannot be 
fully encoded. While it's more of an framework impl detail, I am 
wondering if we should also talk about abbreviations, like 
o.a.k.connect.<someMoreNestePackagesWithLongNames>

Just bringing it up, as the KIP also prescribes how client.framework.id 
should be used in general, so covering this part could also make sense?



> The KIP adds two tagged fields to v6.

Three, not two, right?



> The maximum length of the fields is 249 characters

Should this be "each field" instead of "the fields" which reads as "the 
sum of all field should not exceed 249 chars"?





In the "Clients configuration" section, ie, the table listing the three 
new configs, importance level for client.framework.id is missing. Should 
be LOW.



> if specified, must match the regular expression

What is the purpose to enforce such a pattern? My understanding was, 
that such an encoding is a recommendation only, not something the KIP 
dictates?



We should describe how Kafka Connect and Kafka Streams react, if one of 
the new configs is specified by the user. The KIP only say very high 
level that

> If a framework provides a way for the application to set its own Kafka configs, the framework's own values for the client framework name, version and ID take precedence over the values the application sets for these configs; they are provided to be used by a framework.

But this statement is generic, and not limited to Connect and Streams, 
and instructions how it _should_ work for external frameworks.

For Connect and Streams we should either refuse to startup, ie, Connect 
worker throws ConfigException, deployment of a connector fails (not sure 
if applicable?), and `StreamsConfig` throws, or we ignore the config and 
only log a warning about it. Personally, I think throwing an exception 
might be better.




About Chris comment:

> I wonder if there could be a sort of middle ground between "client" and
> "framework" that would be a good fit for connector type/version--what do
> you think about "application", as in "client.application.name" and
> "client.application.version"? The Kafka Streams equivalents for these would
> be the existing "application.id" property and a new "application.version"
> property. This could also be independently useful for Kafka Streams
> developers who want to track rollouts that update Streams configs and/or
> topologies.

I see his point about Connect, but I am not sure right now, if/how it 
would apply to Streams and if it would indeed be helpful or not... 
Didn't form a full opinion on it yet, but will monitor the discussion 
and follow up as needed.




-Matthias


