Hi Lianet,
Thanks for the massively updated KIP :)

AS13: I suggest adding client.rack to the list of default configs. This one
is surely a source of problems when misconfigured.

AS14: For the RPC responses, please could you list the error codes which
can be returned as comments in the JSON so that they end up in
the source code. In my experience, this seems like the most authoritative
information about the actual error codes for the RPC responses in the code.

AS15: The KIP mentions a future broker-side enforcement capability, which
to me implies that the plugin will be able to throw a PolicyViolationException
and the PushClientConfigsResponse will also carry the POLICY_VIOLATION error
code, at least in the future. But, this KIP says that the configs push
RPC exchange does not block any user API calls. In the future, a policy
violation probably would want to be able to block an API call, at least so
we know how the error would be reported. I wonder whether this KIP ought to
be preparing the ground for what comes next here so we don't get a behaviour
change in the applications just because there's a more capable plugin in
a future broker.

AS16: I'm interested by the idea of logical client configs introduced by
this KIP, but I have questions. Does the broker need to request the logical
client configs in the same way as regular configs (I guess so), implying that
the broker config will need to include those configs in the
client.configs.requested.keys config? Are the acceptable logical client type
values configured in the broker? This area seems a bit incomplete so far.

AS17: Given that the RPCs use RPC request header v3, they cannot have
a ZERO_UUID as the client instance ID. It's specifically prohibited by
KIP-1313.

Thanks,
Andrew

On 2026/10/14 20:13:47 Lianet Magrans wrote:
> Hi all,
>=20
> I'm taking over KIP-1324 and have updated it after a general review,
> considering all the feedback from the previous discussion. The main changes:
>=20
> - this is now operator-driven: the broker defines which configs it requests
> from clients + clients can opt out or exclude keys, and never send
> sensitive or custom configs
> - observability only: the broker hands the configs to an asynchronous
> plugin, which cannot reject or change a client's configuration
> - the client exchange never blocks client operations.
> - Kafka Streams pushes its own snapshot through its admin client, and the
> KIP-1368 framework id groups the snapshots of one Streams instance.
> - builds on top of KIP-1313 for the client instance id.
>=20
> All open questions/suggestions from the previous discussion were
> considered: each one is either integrated into the KIP, covered in Rejected
> Alternatives, or mentioned "out of scope". So to Andrew, Murali, Mickael,
> Apoorv, Matthias, Rajini and Hector, thanks for the feedback, you should
> find all the answers to your questions in the new KIP doc.
>=20
> Please take another look:
> https://cwiki.apache.org/confluence/display/KAFKA/KIP-1324%3A+Support+clien=
t+configuration+observability
>=20
> Thanks all!
> Lianet
