Hi Chris,
Thanks for your reply. Sorry for the delay in responding.

The idea with client.framework.id is that it groups together a bunch of logic=
ally
related client connections. For Kafka Streams, it makes a bunch of producer,
consumer and admin connections, and it would be good to know which are related
to each other. We use client.framework.id for this.

For Kafka Connect, I think that you are correct that Connect worker
ID is the appropriate identifier. I have updated the KIP accordingly. If
there is a useful difference between connections made by the Kafka Connect ru=
ntime
for its own purposes (group management for example) and connections made
by a connector, it's probably worth being able to distinguish those using
this KIP. What makes sense here?

I have changed the KIP so that it disallows setting these new configs in
Kafka Connect configs.

Thanks,
Andrew

On 2026/10/05 23:36:24 Chris Egerton wrote:
> Hi Andrew,
>=20
> I'm not sure about using the instance ID to report connector type and
> version. Concatenating the two and putting them in a single field removes
> structure and forces extra work on users who want to, e.g., view aggregate
> metrics for all versions of a connector. In addition, using it to
> identify unique Kafka Streams processes makes sense, but (to me at least)
> that means the intuitive use for it by Kafka Connect would be to report the
> worker ID (i.e., the advertised URL) or the exact entity that the client is
> used for (connector name for connectors, task ID for tasks). What do you
> think about using the instance ID field to report the Connect worker ID
> instead, since the connector name/task ID are already set in the default
> client IDs used by Kafka Connect?
